>>
endobj
583 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 389 0 R
/E (Equivariance: if you transform the input, the representation transforms in a predictable, corresponding way.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
584 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 389 0 R
/E (If you shift an image right by 10px, the feature map also shifts right by 10px and the structure is preserved.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
585 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 389 0 R
/E (Invariance: The output remains the same regardless of where the input appears.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
586 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 389 0 R
/E (If you shift an image right by 10px, the model still predict the class correctly.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
587 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 389 0 R
/E (But Invariance translation also a bit problematic with cnns as well. So that is why we use data augmentation.)
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
