689 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 409 0 R
/E (Full fine-tuning a BIG model with only 1,500 images is a classic recipe for overfitting. The model will almost certainly overwrite the rich visual representations built from ImageNet.)
/Pg 56 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
690 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 409 0 R
/E (You end up with the worst of both worlds, losing pretrained knowledge without gaining domain expertise.)
/Pg 56 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
691 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 409 0 R
/E (Depending on the domain shift, the smarter move is to freeze the entire backbone and only train the classification head. The pretrained ViT already encodes genuinely useful high-level features like textures, color gradients, edge patterns that could transfer to plant disease detection.)
/Pg 56 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
692 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 409 0 R
/E (Freezing reduces trainable parameters from 86M to roughly 3,000, which dramatically cuts overfitting risk, GPU memory requirements, and training time simultaneously.)
/Pg 56 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
693 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 409 0 R
/E (If head-only training plateaus, I would then gradually unfreeze the last one or two transformer blocks to allow slight domain adaptation. Or maybe I can still keep the whole architecture frozen and add additional layers \(adapter, lora, prompt\) to existing model and train them with the plant dataset that I have. )
/Pg 56 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
